// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  "britons:hunt": "The Welsh laws of Hywel Dda, set down around 940, gave the king's chief huntsman, the pencynydd, a seat in the hall and a share of every hide. A king's trained hound was valued at a full pound, as much as several cows. In autumn the hunters rode out with horn and leash into the oak valleys after the stag, knowing that the meat on the winter tables, and their own honour, rode with them.",
  "britons:battle": "Around the year 600 the men of Gododdin feasted for a year on the mead of their lord at Din Eidyn, then rode south to Catraeth to fight the Angles. The bard Aneirin sang that three hundred went, gold-torqued and laughing, and that hardly any came home. Y Gododdin is not a song of victory. It praises each fallen man by name, so that the debt of mead was paid and the memory kept.",
  "britons:gather": "In Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion the last ears of corn in the field were plaited together and called y wrach, the hag. The reapers stood back and threw their sickles at it in turn; whoever cut it had to carry it to the farmhouse dry, while the women waited at the door with buckets of water. Harvest ended in laughter, a soaking, and a feast that paid the reapers' sweat.",
  "britons:ritual": "On Nos Galan Gaeaf, the eve of winter, Welsh villagers built a bonfire on the hill and each person threw in a white stone marked as their own. They circled the fire, then ran home before the tailless black sow could catch them. At dawn they searched the ashes. A stone that could not be found meant its owner would not see another winter. People went quietly, not in terror, to learn what the year held.",
  "britons:rain": "Imagined: drovers gather with their black cattle above Llandovery, ready for the long road to the English markets. The mist sits thick on the Black Mountains and nobody moves; the cattle could walk off the edge of a cwm. The men sit on the wall, share a pipe, and sing hymns in low voices. Rain is nothing to them. They wait only for the cloud to lift and show the path.",
  "britons:flood": "On the morning of 30 January 1607 a wall of water came up the Bristol Channel and the Severn, drowning the low farms of Gwent and Glamorgan as far as the hills. Some two thousand people died on both shores. In the churches of Goldcliff and Redwick, brass plaques still mark how high the sea came. People said God had spoken; they rebuilt the sea walls all the same.",
  "britons:wind": "On the night of 25 October 1859 a hurricane drove the steam clipper Royal Charter, home from the Australian goldfields, onto the rocks at Moelfre on Anglesey. Some four hundred and fifty died. Men of the village formed a human chain into the surf and pulled survivors ashore. Charles Dickens came to see the place, and wrote of the parson who buried the drowned strangers as if they were his own.",
  "britons:fire": "On 18 September 1400 Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales at Glyndyfrdwy, and within days his men set fire to the English borough of Ruthin. Market stalls and timber houses burned while the castle held. For Welsh people it was the first spark of a long hope after a century of conquest; the bards hailed him, and the smoke over the Clwyd valley was read as the start of something.",
  "britons:thunder": "Imagined: a thunderstorm rolls over Eryri, and the shepherds of the high farms count the strokes as they gather the ewes off the slopes. An old woman says the mountain is angry with the copper miners again. The children are told to cover the mirrors. When the storm passes, a hymn rises from the chapel at the foot of the valley, half gratitude and half the habit of every Sunday.",
  "britons:birth": "On 28 January 1457, in Pembroke Castle, Margaret Beaufort, just thirteen and already a widow, gave birth to a son after a dangerous labour. She never bore another child. The Welsh bards soon sang of a mab darogan, a son of prophecy who would restore the old British crown. Few could have believed the frail boy, Henry Tudor, would end the Wars of the Roses at Bosworth.",
  "britons:death": "In the Welsh borders a sin-eater was sometimes called to a funeral. John Aubrey wrote in the 1680s of a poor man who stood by the coffin, took a loaf, a bowl of beer and a sixpence passed over the corpse, and so took the dead person's sins on himself. The family then sat up through the gwylnos, the wake night, singing hymns until the walk to the churchyard.",

  "english:hunt": "On 2 August 1100 King William II, called Rufus, rode hunting in the New Forest, the royal forest his father had made by clearing villages. As the sun sank, an arrow loosed by Walter Tirel at a stag struck the king in the chest. Tirel fled to France. Charcoal burners carried the body to Winchester on a cart. Many English said the forest had taken its revenge, and few wept.",
  "english:battle": "On 25 October 1415, the feast of Saint Crispin, Henry V's tired, sick army faced a far larger French host at Agincourt. Rain had turned the ploughed field to mud. The longbowmen drove stakes, and as the French men-at-arms floundered toward them, the arrows fell like snow. By evening the French nobility lay dead in heaps. England sang 'Deo gratias Anglia' and remembered it for six hundred years.",
  "english:gather": "Thomas Tusser's 'Five Hundred Points of Good Husbandry' (1573) told English farmers how to bring in the harvest, then how to keep Harvest Home. The last load came in on the hock cart, decked with green boughs and a corn dolly, with reapers riding on top shouting. The farmer gave a supper of beef and plum pudding, and master and labourer drank together, once a year, as equals.",
  "english:ritual": "Before Elizabeth I was crowned, her adviser John Dee, mathematician and astrologer, was asked to cast the stars and choose a day. He chose 15 January 1559. Dee later spent years seeking angelic messages through a scryer, hoping to learn the future of England and the world. For most English people the question went simply to God, in the Book of Common Prayer, Sunday after Sunday.",
  "english:rain": "On 19 July 1588 the Spanish Armada was sighted off the Lizard. Legend says Francis Drake was playing bowls on Plymouth Hoe and finished his game. The truth behind it: the English fleet could not leave harbour against the wind and the incoming tide. There was nothing to do but wait for the ebb and the turn of the wind, and that night they warped their ships out into the Sound.",
  "english:flood": "On the night of 31 January 1953 a storm surge swept over the sea walls of eastern England. On Canvey Island in the Thames estuary, families woke to water rising through the floorboards; fifty-eight died there, and over three hundred along the coast. Neighbours rowed through streets in the dark. The country answered with sandbags and soldiers, and then built the Thames Barrier so it would not happen again.",
  "english:wind": "On 26 and 27 November 1703 the Great Storm crossed southern England. Chimneys fell, the lead was ripped off Westminster Abbey, and thousands of sailors drowned on the Goodwin Sands. Henry Winstanley, who had built the Eddystone lighthouse and wished to be in it during the greatest storm ever, was there that night; nothing of him or it was found. Daniel Defoe collected eyewitness letters and printed them as 'The Storm'.",
  "english:fire": "In the early hours of 2 September 1666 a fire began in Thomas Farriner's bakery in Pudding Lane. A dry east wind drove it through the timber city for four days. Samuel Pepys buried his wine and his Parmesan cheese in his garden and watched St Paul's cathedral burn. Over thirteen thousand houses were lost, yet very few people died, and Wren rebuilt the churches in stone.",
  "english:thunder": "On Sunday 21 October 1638, during afternoon service at Widecombe-in-the-Moor on Dartmoor, a great thunderstorm struck the church. A ball of fire burst through a window, part of the tower crashed through the roof, and four people died. Villagers said the Devil himself had come for a card-playing man in the congregation. Verses recording the day still hang in the church.",
  "english:birth": "On 7 September 1533 Anne Boleyn gave birth to a daughter at Greenwich. Henry VIII had broken with Rome to have a son; the jousts planned to celebrate were quietly cancelled. No one at the christening could have guessed that this girl, Elizabeth, would rule for forty-four years, face down the Armada, and give her name to an age. Her birth disappointed a king and delighted a nation later.",
  "english:death": "In English parishes a passing bell was tolled when someone died: three strokes for a child, six for a woman, nine for a man, the 'nine tailors', then a stroke for each year of their age. Everyone in the fields stopped to count. In 1624 John Donne wrote, 'never send to know for whom the bell tolls; it tolls for thee'. Then came the black cloth, the procession and the churchyard.",

  "house-of-lancaster:hunt": "Between 1406 and 1413 Edward, Duke of York, translated a French hunting book into English as 'The Master of Game' and dedicated it to Prince Henry of Lancaster, the future Henry V. It describes the hart, the boar and the hounds, and claims hunting keeps a man from idleness and sin. The prince loved the chase. Two years after his crowning, Edward died fighting beside him at Agincourt.",
  "house-of-lancaster:battle": "On Palm Sunday, 29 March 1461, the Lancastrian army met the Yorkists at Towton in a snowstorm. The wind blew the snow into Lancastrian faces; their arrows fell short while Yorkist arrows struck home. After hours of slaughter the line broke and men drowned in the Cock Beck. It may have been the bloodiest day ever fought on English soil, and it cost Henry VI his crown.",
  "house-of-lancaster:gather": "Imagined: at Michaelmas the reeves of the Duchy of Lancaster's manors ride to Pontefract with their rolls, the year's rents in coin and kind. Bushels of oats, cartloads of wool and fat geese crowd the castle yard. The steward tallies them on notched sticks. The money will pay for archers and for the king's wars in France; the villagers hope only that it is enough to keep the soldiers away.",
  "house-of-lancaster:ritual": "On the night of 20 March 1413, as his father Henry IV lay dead, the new king Henry V went alone to Westminster Abbey and spent the night with a holy recluse in his cell. Chroniclers said he came out a changed man, leaving the wild companions of his youth behind. He was asking God what kind of king he should be, and he never told anyone the answer he received.",
  "house-of-lancaster:rain": "In the summer of 1485 Henry Tudor, the last hope of the Lancastrian cause, waited at Harfleur with a small army of exiles and French soldiers. Everything hung on the wind. On 1 August it turned, and his ships sailed west; on 7 August he landed at Mill Bay near Milford Haven, kneeling to recite a psalm on Welsh soil. Within three weeks he was king.",
  "house-of-lancaster:flood": "In October 1483 the Duke of Buckingham rose against Richard III in favour of Henry Tudor. But days of rain swelled the Severn so high that his army could not cross; the flood drowned cattle and children in their cradles, and was long remembered as Buckingham's Great Water. His men melted away, and he was betrayed and beheaded at Salisbury. Lancastrian hopes waited two more years.",
  "house-of-lancaster:wind": "Also in October 1483, Henry Tudor sailed from Brittany to join the rising. A storm scattered his fleet in the Channel. When his ship at last reached Poole, he saw soldiers on the shore inviting him to land as friends. He suspected a trap, raised sail, and turned back to France. The storm had cost him the rebellion, and possibly saved his life.",
  "house-of-lancaster:fire": "On 13 June 1381, rebels of the Peasants' Revolt broke into the Savoy Palace, the great London house of John of Gaunt, father of the House of Lancaster. They hated him as the power behind the boy king. They smashed his silver, threw his jewels into the Thames, and burned the palace to the ground; a man caught stealing was thrown into the flames by the rebels themselves. Nothing was rebuilt for a century.",
  "house-of-lancaster:thunder": "In 1402 Henry IV led an army into Wales against Owain Glyndŵr. In September a storm of wind, hail and thunder broke over the camp so violently that the king's tent was blown down and a lance nearly killed him. English chroniclers said Glyndŵr had raised the storm by magic. The army retreated, soaked and hungry, and the Lancastrian king learned that Wales would not be won quickly.",
  "house-of-lancaster:birth": "On 6 December 1421 Catherine of Valois gave birth to a son at Windsor. Henry V was away at the siege of Meaux. A later chronicle says he had warned against Windsor: Henry of Monmouth would reign short and win much, Henry of Windsor would reign long and lose all. Within a year the father was dead, and the baby was king of England and France. The saying came true.",
  "house-of-lancaster:death": "Henry V died at Vincennes on 31 August 1422, aged thirty-five. His body was carried slowly across France to Calais, with a painted leather effigy in royal robes lying on the coffin, and torches burning around it at every town. In England crowds lined the road to London. He was buried in Westminster Abbey on 7 November, his helm and saddle hung above the grave.",

  "celts:hunt": "The Greek writer Arrian, around 130 AD, admired how the Celts hunted hare: not with nets, but on horseback behind swift hounds they called vertragi, for the joy of the chase. He records that they set aside a coin for every hare, fox or deer they killed, and once a year spent the hoard on a sacrifice and feast for their goddess of the hunt, with the hounds crowned with flowers.",
  "celts:battle": "At Telamon in 225 BC the Gauls faced Rome between two Roman armies. Polybius wrote that the Gaesatae warriors stood in the front rank naked but for gold torcs and armbands, and that the din of their countless horns and carnyxes, with the whole army shouting, seemed to come from the land itself. The Romans were afraid; yet the Gauls were crushed, and their torcs carried to the Capitol.",
  "celts:gather": "At Lughnasa, the start of harvest at the beginning of August, Irish communities climbed hills to pick the first bilberries, cut the first sheaf, and hold games and matchmaking in honour of the god Lugh. Later, Christians kept the climb as Garland Sunday or Reek Sunday, going barefoot up Croagh Patrick. The first fruits were a sign that hunger would end and the year would turn out well.",
  "celts:ritual": "Early Irish texts describe the tarbfeis, the bull-feast held to learn who should be king of Tara. A white bull was killed, and one man ate his fill of its flesh and drank the broth, then lay down while four druids chanted over him. Whoever he saw in his sleep would be king; had he lied, it was said, his lips would destroy him. The future was dreamed, gravely, on behalf of all.",
  "celts:rain": "Imagined: on a western sea loch of the Highlands, the boats of a clan lie drawn up on the shingle, ready to row to the islands for a gathering. A grey haar sits over the water and swallows the headland. The piper plays a slow air to pass the time; the old men tell the young ones this is no rain at all. By noon a breeze from the hills tears the mist apart.",
  "celts:flood": "Aristotle and Ephorus both repeated a story about the Celts of the northern coasts: that they feared nothing, and when the great sea-tides came in over their land they took up their weapons and marched into the waves to meet them, until many were drowned. The Greeks called it rashness. To the Celts, it may have been honour: one did not run from anything, not even the sea.",
  "celts:wind": "Adomnán's Life of Saint Columba, written about 700 on Iona, tells how the saint's monks, caught by a great storm in the whirlpool of Corryvreckan between the islands, were saved when Columba, far away on Iona, knelt and prayed for them. Island people at sea in skin-covered currachs lived close to such storms, and every crossing began with a blessing and ended with thanks.",
  "celts:fire": "Cormac's Glossary, written in Ireland around 900, says that at Beltaine, the first of May, druids lit two great fires with incantations, and the cattle were driven between them to protect them from disease for the coming year. In the Highlands and Ireland the custom of the May fire lasted into modern times: hearths were put out and relit from the sacred flame, and the summer grazing began.",
  "celts:thunder": "In 335 BC Alexander the Great met Celtic envoys from the Adriatic on the Danube. He asked what they feared most, expecting them to say himself. They answered that they feared nothing, except that the sky might fall on their heads. Their god Taranis rode the thunder with his wheel, and only the heavens breaking open could shake men who were otherwise unafraid.",
  "celts:birth": "Before Columba was born in 521 at Gartan in Donegal, his mother Eithne dreamed that an angel brought her a cloak of every colour, which then rose into the air and spread over the mountains and the lakes of Ireland and Scotland. The angel told her that her son would lead countless souls to heaven. The child of a noble family would found Iona and carry his faith to the Picts.",
  "celts:death": "Irish wakes lasted through the night with candles, pipes of tobacco, stories and games; then the women raised the caoineadh, the keen, a sung lament of grief and praise. In 1773 Eibhlín Dubh Ní Chonaill found her husband Art Ó Laoghaire shot dead, and her keen for him became one of the great poems of the Irish language, full of love, fury and the blood on her hands.",

  "franks:hunt": "The Frankish king Theudebert I, grandson of Clovis, loved to hunt the great wild beasts of the forests. The Byzantine historian Agathias says that in 548 a huge wild bull, an aurochs, charged him; a tree branch it struck fell on the king and wounded him, and he died soon afterwards. Hunting the bison and the aurochs was a royal privilege, and a royal danger.",
  "franks:battle": "In October 732, near Tours and Poitiers, the Frankish army of Charles Martel stood in a close shield wall against the cavalry of the Umayyad governor Abd al-Rahman. A Mozarabic chronicle of 754 says the men of the north stood 'like a wall of ice', unmoving, and cut down the Arabs with their swords. Abd al-Rahman fell; at night his army withdrew south, and Charles was called the Hammer.",
  "franks:gather": "Around 800 Charlemagne issued the Capitulare de villis, rules for his royal estates. It ordered each steward to keep good records, to make wine and beer cleanly, to keep bees and fish ponds, and to grow some ninety listed plants: lilies, roses, sage, rue, cucumbers, apples, pears. Imagined behind it is the steward's anxiety, counting the barrels before the emperor's household arrives to eat them.",
  "franks:ritual": "Gregory of Tours records how, around 555, the clergy of Dijon sought the future of the rebellious prince Chramn. They placed three books on the altar, the Prophets, the Apostle and the Gospels, prayed that God would show what would happen, and each was opened at random. All three readings warned of ruin. They were not wrong: in 560 Chramn was burned with his family by his own father.",
  "franks:rain": "Each spring, on the Rogation days before Ascension, Frankish clergy and villagers walked the bounds of their fields behind the cross, chanting litanies and asking God for good weather and an end to frost and drought. The custom spread from Vienne, where Bishop Mamertus began it in the 470s after earthquakes and fires. Then they went home and waited for the sky to answer.",
  "franks:flood": "Gregory of Tours wrote that in 580 such heavy rains fell for twelve days in the Auvergne that the rivers burst their banks and drowned fields, people and cattle; the Loire and the Allier swept away houses and the harvest. He listed it among the signs of an anxious year, with an earthquake, a plague and strange lights in the sky. Floods to him were warnings as well as disasters.",
  "franks:wind": "Imagined: in winter at Aachen, the north wind howls through the new palace, rattling the lead roof of the Palatine Chapel. Monks of the palace school bend over their wax tablets by candlelight while servants block the doors with straw. The emperor, Einhard tells us, liked to swim in the hot springs; tonight the steam is torn away by the gale, and the old men talk of the pagan gods of storm.",
  "franks:fire": "Einhard tells how Charlemagne built a great wooden bridge over the Rhine at Mainz, five hundred paces long, which took ten years to complete. In 813, the year before the emperor's death, it caught fire and burned to the waterline in three hours, so that not a single plank was left above the water. His court remembered it later as one of the signs that his end was near.",
  "franks:thunder": "Among the omens before Charlemagne's death in 814, Einhard lists that lightning struck the golden apple on the roof of his church at Aachen and hurled it onto the bishop's house nearby. Eclipses and earthquakes shook the palace, and the inscription naming Charles as the builder faded until it could hardly be read. The emperor himself ignored all of it, Einhard says.",
  "franks:birth": "Gregory of Tours records that Queen Clotilde, a Christian, had her first son by the pagan King Clovis baptised; the baby died in his white robes. Clovis blamed her God. When a second son, Chlodomer, was born she baptised him too, and he fell ill. She prayed, and the child lived. The king took notice. Not long afterwards, around 496, Clovis himself was baptised at Reims.",
  "franks:death": "In 1653 workmen in Tournai dug up the grave of Childeric I, father of Clovis, who died around 481. He lay with his sword, a gold seal ring showing his long hair, a cloak sewn with some three hundred golden bees, and a horse's head; later digs found whole horses buried around him. His people had sent their king to the next world as a warrior, with everything he needed.",

  "french:hunt": "On 14 July 1789, the day the Bastille fell, Louis XVI wrote one word in his diary: 'Rien', nothing. He meant there had been no kill in the hunt that day. Hunting was his passion and his comfort; he recorded every stag and boar for years. The word has become a symbol of a king who did not see his world ending, but it also shows a shy man who loved the forests of Versailles.",
  "french:battle": "On 20 September 1792 the ragged army of the new French Republic faced the Prussians at Valmy. The cannonade went on for hours; the French did not break, and General Kellermann raised his hat on his sword and cried 'Vive la Nation!' The Prussians withdrew. Goethe, watching from their side, told the officers that from this place and this day began a new era in world history.",
  "french:gather": "French law and custom long gave the poor the right of glanage: after the harvest was carried off, they could walk the stubble and pick up the ears the reapers had left. In 1857 Jean-François Millet painted three women stooping in the field at Chailly, the wealthy harvest piled in the distance. Critics in Paris found it dangerous; the women themselves only hoped for enough bread for the winter.",
  "french:ritual": "In 1555 Michel de Nostradamus published his first Prophecies, and Queen Catherine de' Medici summoned him to the court to cast horoscopes for her children. When Henri II died in 1559 from a lance splinter in a tournament, people read one quatrain as having foretold it. The Catholic Church disapproved of fortune-telling, but the anxious queen and many French readers kept asking about the future.",
  "french:rain": "When drought or endless rain threatened the harvest, Parisians brought out the reliquary of Sainte Geneviève, patroness of the city. In great processions, recorded many times from the Middle Ages to the 1700s, it was carried down from her abbey to Notre-Dame with the shrine of Saint Marcel, barefoot monks and all the guilds following. Then the city waited, praying, for the weather to change.",
  "french:flood": "In January 1910 the Seine rose for days until it stood more than eight metres above normal. Water filled the Métro and the cellars, and Parisians crossed their streets on planks and in rowing boats. People watched the stone Zouave soldier on the Pont de l'Alma: the water reached his shoulders. The city did not panic; cafés stayed open, and neighbours fed each other until the river fell.",
  "french:wind": "On 26 and 27 December 1999 two great storms, Lothar and Martin, swept across France with winds over 170 km/h. Millions lost power at Christmas, and some ninety people died. In the park of Versailles about ten thousand trees fell, including ones planted under Marie-Antoinette. Afterwards people from all over the world sent money to replant the gardens, as if mourning old friends.",
  "french:fire": "On the evening of 15 April 2019 the roof of Notre-Dame de Paris caught fire. Crowds gathered along the Seine and watched the spire collapse into the flames. Many knelt and sang 'Je vous salue Marie' into the night. Firefighters saved the bell towers and the relics. Five years later, on 7 December 2024, the cathedral reopened, its bells ringing again over Paris.",
  "french:thunder": "On 10 May 1752 at Marly-la-Ville, following Benjamin Franklin's idea, Thomas-François Dalibard set up a tall iron rod during a thunderstorm. An old soldier left to watch it saw sparks leap from the rod with a crackle, and ran to fetch the village priest. It was the first proof that lightning is electricity. Thunder, the voice of heaven, had become something the French could study.",
  "french:birth": "Louis XIII and Anne of Austria had been married for twenty-three years without a living child when, on 5 September 1638, a son was born at Saint-Germain-en-Laye. The whole kingdom rang its bells and called him Louis Dieudonné, the gift of God. No one knew that this boy, Louis XIV, would reign for seventy-two years, build Versailles, and make France both glorious and exhausted.",
  "french:death": "On 15 December 1840 the remains of Napoleon, brought back from Saint Helena, were carried through Paris to the Invalides. The cold was bitter and snow fell, yet hundreds of thousands lined the Champs-Élysées. Old soldiers of the Grande Armée wept in their faded uniforms. He had asked to rest 'on the banks of the Seine, among the French people I loved so much'.",

  "jeanne-darc:hunt": "On 18 June 1429, after Orléans, Jeanne's army was searching for the retreating English near Patay. The English longbowmen lay hidden in a hedge. Then a stag broke from the woods and ran straight into their lines, and the archers raised a hunting cry. The French scouts heard it, the cavalry charged before the stakes were set, and the English army was destroyed. People said God had sent the stag.",
  "jeanne-darc:battle": "On 7 May 1429 the French stormed Les Tourelles, the English fort guarding the bridge of Orléans. Jeanne was struck by an arrow above her breast while setting a ladder, and was carried away in tears. She had the arrow drawn, prayed, and returned with her banner. As the soldiers saw it touch the wall they surged forward, and the fort fell. The next morning the English lifted the siege.",
  "jeanne-darc:gather": "At her trial in Rouen in 1431 the judges asked Jeanne whether she had learned a trade. She said yes: she had learned to sew and spin from her mother, and she feared no woman of Rouen at it. At Domrémy she had helped in the house, led the animals to pasture and worked in the fields at harvest. It was a plain village life she gave up for the war.",
  "jeanne-darc:ritual": "Jeanne told her judges that when she was about thirteen she heard a voice from God in her father's garden at Domrémy, at noon in summer, from the side of the church, with a great light. Later Saint Michael, Saint Catherine and Saint Margaret spoke to her, often when the bells rang. They told her the future she must make: to raise the siege of Orléans and see the Dauphin crowned.",
  "jeanne-darc:rain": "When Jeanne reached Orléans on 29 April 1429, the supply boats could not sail upstream: the wind blew against them. The commander Dunois testified later that she told him to wait, and that suddenly, as if at her word, the wind changed and the boats sailed with full sails past the English forts. He said from that moment he had hope in her.",
  "jeanne-darc:flood": "Imagined: spring at Domrémy, and the Meuse spills over its meadows after the thaw. The village children are kept from the water, and the men wade out to bring the sheep to higher ground near the Bois Chenu. Jeanne, a girl among them, helps carry lambs. In the evening the village gathers in the little church of Saint Remy and gives thanks that the water stopped short of the houses.",
  "jeanne-darc:wind": "Imagined: in the winter of 1430, Jeanne is held in the tower of Beaurevoir castle, prisoner of the Burgundians, waiting to be sold to the English. A storm batters the walls and the wind whistles through the shutters. She hears that Compiègne, which she promised to help, is still besieged. Despairing of waiting, she leaps from the tower, and survives, to everyone's astonishment.",
  "jeanne-darc:fire": "On 30 May 1431 Jeanne was burned in the Old Market at Rouen. She asked for a cross; an English soldier tied two sticks together and gave it to her, and a priest held up the crucifix from the church so she could see it through the smoke. She called the name of Jesus again and again. Witnesses said many wept, and an English secretary cried, 'We have burned a saint.'",
  "jeanne-darc:thunder": "Imagined: on the road to Reims in July 1429 a summer storm breaks over the army, and thunder rolls across the wheatfields of Champagne. Soldiers who have sworn all their lives look at the Maid and fall silent, because she forbids their oaths. Jeanne rides on under the rain with her banner furled. By evening the sky clears over Troyes, and the men say it is a good sign for the coronation.",
  "jeanne-darc:birth": "Jeanne was born around 1412 in Domrémy, a village on the border of Lorraine, to Jacques d'Arc, a farmer, and Isabelle Romée. People in France told an old prophecy, said to be Merlin's: a maid would come from the oak wood of the Lorraine marches to save the kingdom. Her parents could not imagine it was their daughter, and later fiercely opposed her leaving home.",
  "jeanne-darc:death": "After the burning, the English had Jeanne's ashes thrown into the Seine so that nothing would be left to venerate. In 1456 a retrial, begun at the plea of her old mother Isabelle Romée, declared the verdict null. Isabelle stood in Notre-Dame of Paris and asked for justice for her daughter. In 1920, nearly five centuries later, Jeanne was declared a saint.",

  "knights-templar:hunt": "The Templar Rule, approved in 1129, forbade the brothers to hunt with hawks or with hounds, or to shout in the chase, for such amusements were worldly. It made one exception: a knight might hunt the lion, 'which goes about seeking whom it may devour', for that was the enemy of God. Their hunting was meant to be war on evil, not sport.",
  "knights-templar:battle": "On 4 July 1187 the army of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, maddened by thirst, was trapped by Saladin at the Horns of Hattin. The Templars charged again and again, but the army was destroyed and the True Cross captured. Afterwards Saladin had about two hundred captured Templars and Hospitallers executed. Their Master, Gerard de Ridefort, who had urged the march, was spared.",
  "knights-templar:gather": "The Templars' fighting in the East was paid for by thousands of farms in Europe. At Cressing Temple in Essex their brothers built two great timber barns in the 1200s, still standing, to hold the harvest of wheat and barley. Lay brothers and hired reapers worked the fields; the profit was shipped to Acre. To a farmhand there, the Holy Land was something paid for in sheaves.",
  "knights-templar:ritual": "A man joining the Temple was received at night, in chapter, before the gathered brothers. He was asked whether he had debts, a wife, or illness, and warned that he would give up his own will entirely. He swore obedience, chastity and poverty, received the white mantle with the red cross, and the brothers sang Psalm 133. What future God gave him was no longer his to ask.",
  "knights-templar:rain": "Imagined: in the spring of 1250 a ship of Templar recruits waits in the harbour of Marseille for the passage season. Every morning the captain sniffs the air, and every morning the mistral blows the wrong way. The young knights pray in the Templar chapel by the port and polish their mail. When at last the wind turns, they cross themselves and sail for Acre, not knowing they will never return.",
  "knights-templar:flood": "Imagined: in winter the Jordan rises brown and fast at the pilgrims' fords. Templar knights, sworn to protect travellers on the road from Jerusalem to the river, ride down to warn a band of pilgrims not to cross. The pilgrims weep; they came so far to bathe where Christ was baptised. The knights camp beside them and keep watch until the water falls and the crossing is safe.",
  "knights-templar:wind": "In May 1291 the Mamluks broke into Acre, the last great city of the crusaders. The Templars held their fortress by the sea for days while ships took refugees away. A storm-swept sea made the escape deadly; women and children drowned in the harbour. On 28 May the walls of the Temple collapsed, killing defenders and attackers together. The Order's treasure sailed north to Sidon with Tibald Gaudin.",
  "knights-templar:fire": "On 18 March 1314 Jacques de Molay, last Grand Master of the Temple, and Geoffroi de Charney were burned alive on a small island in the Seine, facing Notre-Dame. They had retracted their confessions, declaring the Order innocent. De Molay asked to be tied facing the cathedral with his hands free to pray. The crowd was moved by their calm, and some gathered their ashes as relics.",
  "knights-templar:thunder": "Imagined: in the castle of Safed in Galilee, rebuilt by the Templars in the 1240s, a night thunderstorm breaks over the walls. The brothers rise for matins as the Rule demands, and say their paternosters in the dark chapel while lightning shows the hills below. Not one may leave his place or speak needlessly. When the storm passes, the watch reports the road is quiet.",
  "knights-templar:birth": "The Templars were monks and could have no children; their Rule even forbade them to stand as godfathers. Their own birth came in 1129 at the Council of Troyes, when Hugues de Payens and a handful of poor knights who guarded pilgrims received a Rule and the backing of Bernard of Clairvaux. Few guessed this little brotherhood would grow rich and powerful across all Christendom.",
  "knights-templar:death": "The Templar Rule set out what to do when a brother died. For seven days every brother said a hundred paternosters for his soul, and for forty days his portion of food and drink was given to a poor man. A dead brother was buried at the house where he died, in his habit. There was no grief displayed; a knight of the Temple went to God with his brothers praying.",

  "burgundians:hunt": "At the park of Hesdin in Artois, the dukes of Burgundy hunted deer in a walled forest, and kept a gallery of mechanical tricks to surprise their guests: statues that squirted water, floors that dropped visitors into sacks of feathers, and a machine that made thunder and rain. Philip the Good restored it in the 1430s. A hunt there ended in laughter, wet clothes and a feast.",
  "burgundians:battle": "On 2 March 1476 Charles the Bold's army met the Swiss at Grandson. As the Swiss main body came over the hill, the great horns of Uri and Unterwalden, called the Bull and the Cow, bellowed across the valley. The Burgundians panicked and fled, leaving behind the duke's tents, tapestries, gold and diamonds. The Swiss divided the treasure; the duke's pride never recovered.",
  "burgundians:gather": "In 1395 Duke Philip the Bold banned the 'disloyal' Gamay grape from the Côte d'Or, ordering only Pinot noir to be planted, and so shaped Burgundy wine forever. In 1443 the chancellor Nicolas Rolin founded the Hôtel-Dieu at Beaune to care for the poor, funded by gifts of vineyards. At harvest the pickers knew their grapes would buy bread and beds for the sick.",
  "burgundians:ritual": "On 17 February 1454 at Lille, Philip the Good held the Feast of the Pheasant. After the fall of Constantinople, a live pheasant decked in jewels was brought in, and the duke and his knights swore great vows on it to go on crusade, in an atmosphere half chivalric game, half sincere prayer. They asked God for the future of Christendom. The crusade never sailed.",
  "burgundians:rain": "Imagined: autumn in Dijon, and the rain has fallen for nine days. The grape harvest waits on the slopes of the Côte, swelling and splitting. The vignerons gather in the church of Notre-Dame and look at the sky between Masses. At last a north wind comes down from the plateau and dries the hills. Within a day every family is in the rows, cutting.",
  "burgundians:flood": "Imagined: in the Burgundian Low Countries in 1446, a storm tide breaks dikes in Flanders and Zeeland. The duke's officers ride out to count the drowned villages and the lost rents. In Bruges, merchants watch the canals rise and wonder whether their warehouses will survive. When the water recedes, the duke grants tax relief, and the peasants begin again to wall out the sea.",
  "burgundians:wind": "Imagined: in June 1468 Margaret of York crosses from Margate to Sluis to marry Charles the Bold. A summer gale tosses the fleet, and her ladies are sick and frightened. When at last the ships enter the Zwin, the Burgundian court waits with music and jewels. Days of feasting follow at Bruges, with a whale-shaped float and a unicorn. The storm becomes a story told at table.",
  "burgundians:fire": "In 1468 the city of Liège rebelled against the Duke of Burgundy. Charles the Bold took it by storm, and ordered it burned. For weeks his men set fire to street after street, sparing only the churches; citizens were drowned in the Meuse in pairs. The French king Louis XI, forced to watch, said nothing. The proud city remembered the burning for generations.",
  "burgundians:thunder": "Imagined: in summer 1475 a thunderstorm breaks over the Burgundian camp at the siege of Neuss on the Rhine. Lightning strikes near the ducal tents, and the soldiers kneel in the mud. The siege has lasted almost a year. Some say God is against the duke; Charles the Bold orders the cannons fired back at the storm. Within weeks he abandons the siege.",
  "burgundians:birth": "On 13 February 1457 Isabelle of Bourbon gave birth to a daughter in Brussels. Her husband, the future Charles the Bold, had hoped for a son; there would be no other child. Twenty years later, when Charles fell at Nancy, this girl, Mary of Burgundy, inherited all his lands. She married Maximilian of Austria, and her grandson became Emperor Charles V.",
  "burgundians:death": "For the tomb of Philip the Bold, who died in 1404, Claus Sluter and his followers carved forty little marble mourners, the pleurants, walking in procession beneath the duke's effigy, their faces hidden by hoods, each grieving differently. They show how the Burgundian court buried its dukes: endless black-clad processions, chants, candles, and grief made into art. They still walk today in Dijon.",

  "dutch:hunt": "From 1619 Dutch whalers summered at Smeerenburg, 'Blubber Town', on an island off Spitsbergen. Men rowed out in open sloops after the Greenland right whale, harpooned it, towed it to shore and boiled the blubber in great copper kettles. Hundreds of men worked there amid smoke and stench under the midnight sun. Many died of cold and scurvy, and their graves still lie on the Arctic shore.",
  "dutch:battle": "In June 1667, during the Second Anglo-Dutch War, Michiel de Ruyter's fleet sailed up the Medway, broke the chain across the river at Chatham and burned the English warships at anchor. They towed home the flagship Royal Charles as a trophy; her carved stern is still in the Rijksmuseum. In London Samuel Pepys feared the end; in the Republic the bells rang for days.",
  "dutch:gather": "In 1612 the Beemster lake north of Amsterdam was pumped dry by forty-three windmills, and the bottom was laid out in a perfect grid of fields, roads and ditches. Merchants who had invested watched the first wheat and cattle on land that had been water. To the Dutch, harvesting the polder was a sober miracle: land made by their own hands, with God's blessing.",
  "dutch:ritual": "Dutch Calvinists did not try to read the future in stars or cards; they prayed. On the Biddag voor Gewas en Arbeid, the Day of Prayer for Crops and Labour on the second Wednesday of March, farmers and fishermen in the Reformed churches asked God to bless the coming season. In Bible Belt villages it is still kept, with school out and the church full, solemn and hopeful.",
  "dutch:rain": "In October 1688 William of Orange's invasion fleet waited at Hellevoetsluis for an east wind to carry it to England. A first attempt was driven back by storms. On 11 November the wind turned east, the 'Protestant wind', which carried the Dutch ships down the Channel while it kept the English fleet pinned in the Thames. Dutch ministers had prayed for exactly that.",
  "dutch:flood": "On the night of 18 to 19 November 1421, the Saint Elizabeth's flood broke the dikes of the Grote Waard in Holland. Some twenty villages vanished; the land became the Biesbosch marsh. A story says that after the storm a wooden cradle floated ashore with a baby asleep in it, kept balanced by a cat jumping from side to side. The place became Kinderdijk, the children's dike.",
  "dutch:wind": "On the night of 31 January 1953 a northwest storm drove the North Sea against Zeeland and South Holland. At high tide dikes broke in scores of places; 1,836 people drowned, many in their beds. Survivors waited on roofs for days. The Dutch called it the Watersnoodramp, and answered with the Delta Works, vowing that the wind and sea would never take them like that again.",
  "dutch:fire": "On 7 July 1652 Amsterdam's old Town Hall burned down. Among the crowd watching was the young Jan van der Heyden, who never forgot the helpless bucket chains. In 1672 he and his brother invented the fire engine with a flexible leather hose, and Amsterdam's fires became far less deadly. He later painted the new Town Hall, in sharp quiet detail, as if to make sure it stayed.",
  "dutch:thunder": "On 12 October 1654 a gunpowder store in Delft exploded with a roar heard as far as Texel. Some forty tonnes of powder flattened a quarter of the city and killed over a hundred people, among them Carel Fabritius, painter of the Goldfinch, in his studio. The Delftse Donderslag, the Delft Thunderclap, was taken as God's warning; the city rebuilt, and Vermeer painted its quiet streets.",
  "dutch:birth": "On 14 November 1650, eight days after his father William II died of smallpox, a son was born to Mary Stuart in The Hague. The regents of Holland, glad to be rid of the Orange stadholders, kept the baby from power for years. Many Dutch people still hoped in him. In 1672, the Disaster Year, he saved the Republic, and in 1689 he became William III of England.",
  "dutch:death": "Admiral Michiel de Ruyter was mortally wounded off Sicily in 1676. His embalmed body was brought home, and on 18 March 1677 he was buried in the Nieuwe Kerk of Amsterdam with a great public funeral. Thousands lined the streets and canals. The Republic honoured him with a marble tomb, but sober Dutch preachers reminded the mourners that he had been a humble, praying man.",

  "spanish:hunt": "Philip IV was a passionate hunter, and Velázquez painted him in plain brown hunting clothes with his gun and dog for the hunting lodge of Torre de la Parada near Madrid. Royal hunts were held in great enclosures of canvas, the tela, where deer and boar were driven past the king and court. The montería was spectacle and politics; the beaters only hoped the king shot straight.",
  "spanish:battle": "On 7 October 1571 the Holy League fleet under Don Juan of Austria met the Ottoman fleet at Lepanto. A young soldier, Miguel de Cervantes, was ill with fever but insisted on fighting; he was shot twice in the chest and lost the use of his left hand. He later called it 'the most noble occasion that past or present ages have seen', and was proud of it all his life.",
  "spanish:gather": "Each winter the olive pickers of Jaén spread nets beneath the trees and beat the branches with long poles, gathering the olives for the mills. They were landless day labourers, and the great estates owned the trees. In 1937 the poet Miguel Hernández wrote 'Aceituneros', asking proud Andalusians of Jaén who had raised the olive trees. It became a hymn of the province.",
  "spanish:ritual": "In 1588, while the Armada sailed against England, Philip II ordered prayers in every church of Spain, and at the Escorial monks prayed in shifts day and night before the Blessed Sacrament. The king himself spent hours kneeling, asking God for victory. When the news of disaster came, he is said to have answered calmly that he had sent his ships to fight men, not the winds.",
  "spanish:rain": "In the dry heart of Castile and Andalusia, when drought lasted too long, towns held rogativas pro pluvia: processions carrying the image of their patron saint or Virgin through the fields, praying for rain. Some images were taken from their shrine and not returned until rain fell. Farmers walked behind in silence, looking up at a white sky, waiting for the world to change.",
  "spanish:flood": "On 14 October 1957 days of rain sent the river Turia surging through Valencia. Water rose over five metres in streets near the old city; at least eighty people died. Neighbours sheltered on upper floors and roofs. The city decided never to let the river through again: it was diverted south, and its old bed became the Jardín del Turia, a long green park through Valencia.",
  "spanish:wind": "In September 1588, after their defeat in the Channel, the ships of the Armada sailed around Scotland and Ireland. Atlantic storms drove some two dozen onto the Irish coast. Captain Francisco de Cuéllar, shipwrecked at Streedagh in County Sligo, survived and wrote of crawling ashore among a thousand corpses, then being hidden by Irish chiefs, before he made his way home to Spain.",
  "spanish:fire": "On Christmas Eve 1734 a fire broke out in the old Alcázar palace in Madrid. It burned for four days. Servants and monks smashed windows and threw paintings out into the snow to save them; Velázquez's Las Meninas was among those rescued, but some five hundred works were lost. Philip V built the new Royal Palace on the site, entirely in stone, so it could not burn again.",
  "spanish:thunder": "Imagined: in summer 1640 a thunderstorm breaks over the plain of La Mancha. In the village of Campo de Criptana the miller locks the sails of his windmill and the church bells ring against the storm, as was the custom, to drive away hail. Farmers kneel among the vines. When the hail spares their grapes, they gather in the church to give thanks to the Cristo of the village.",
  "spanish:birth": "On 6 November 1661 Queen Mariana gave birth to a son in Madrid, days after the death of the king's elder boy. The child, Carlos, was frail, the fruit of generations of royal cousins marrying. All Spain prayed he would live and give an heir. He did reign for thirty-five years as Carlos II, called the Bewitched, but died childless, and Europe went to war over his throne.",
  "spanish:death": "Spanish kings are buried in the Pantheon of Kings beneath the high altar of the Escorial, built under Philip II. But first the bodies rest for decades in a sealed chamber, the pudridero, until they are reduced to bones. Only then are they placed in the marble urns. It is a severe, patient custom of Spanish Catholic faith: humility in death before the long wait for Judgment.",

  "portuguese:hunt": "From the 1700s until 1987, men of the Azores hunted sperm whales from open wooden boats with hand harpoons. A lookout on a hill sent up a rocket when he saw a whale's spout, and men left fields and shops and ran to the boats at Lajes do Pico. It was dangerous and poor work, with prayers to the saints before launching. Today the old lookouts watch whales for tourists.",
  "portuguese:battle": "On 14 August 1385 at Aljubarrota a small Portuguese army under King João I and the constable Nuno Álvares Pereira defeated a much larger Castilian army that had come to claim the throne. The Portuguese dug ditches and pits, and English archers helped them. Legend says the baker woman Brites de Almeida killed seven fleeing Castilians with her bread paddle. Portugal stayed independent.",
  "portuguese:gather": "In the Douro valley the grape harvest for port wine was carried down the steep terraces in heavy baskets by men in single file. At night the grapes were trodden barefoot in stone lagares, the treaders linked arm in arm, marching in rhythm to an accordion, a drum, and the foreman's call. Tired, they sang. It was hard work, but also a festival of the whole year's labour.",
  "portuguese:ritual": "On the night of 7 July 1497 Vasco da Gama and his crews kept vigil in the little chapel at Restelo, by the Tagus, praying for their voyage to India. The next morning they walked to the boats in procession, carrying candles, while priests chanted and the crowd wept, many believing they would never see them again. Two years later, fewer than half returned.",
  "portuguese:rain": "King Sebastião vanished at the battle of Alcácer Quibir in Morocco on 4 August 1578, and his body was never clearly found. Portugal soon fell under Spanish rule. For centuries many Portuguese believed that he would return on a foggy morning to save his country. Sebastianism became a feeling of saudade and hope: waiting for the fog to lift and the hidden king to ride out.",
  "portuguese:flood": "On All Saints' Day, 1 November 1755, a great earthquake struck Lisbon during morning Mass. Survivors ran to the open space of the waterfront, and saw the sea draw back, exposing the riverbed, before a tsunami swept in over the quays and the Praça do Comércio. Thousands drowned. Across Europe, people asked how God could allow this, and Voltaire wrote a poem of anger.",
  "portuguese:wind": "In 1488 Bartolomeu Dias was driven far south by a storm that lasted thirteen days. When it ended, he sailed east and found he had passed the southern tip of Africa. On his way home he saw the cape and named it the Cape of Storms. King João II renamed it the Cape of Good Hope, because it opened the sea road to India. The storm had shown the way.",
  "portuguese:fire": "The 1755 earthquake struck on All Saints' Day, when churches were full of candles. The candles fell, and fires burned across Lisbon for five or six days, consuming palaces, libraries and the Royal Opera. When asked what to do, the Marquis of Pombal is said to have answered, 'Bury the dead and feed the living'. He rebuilt the Baixa in straight streets.",
  "portuguese:thunder": "In Camões's epic Os Lusíadas of 1572, as da Gama's fleet nears the Cape of Good Hope a dark cloud rises, the sea roars, and the giant Adamastor appears, the spirit of the cape. In a voice like thunder he foretells the shipwrecks and deaths that await the Portuguese on this route. The sailors listen in terror, but sail on. Many readers heard their own history in it.",
  "portuguese:birth": "On 4 March 1394, in Porto, Philippa of Lancaster gave birth to her third son, Henrique. The country was newly independent and uncertain. No one knew this prince would be remembered as Henry the Navigator, sponsoring voyages down the coast of Africa that changed the world, for good and ill. His mother, it is said, gave each son a sword before her death.",
  "portuguese:death": "Inês de Castro, beloved of Prince Pedro, was killed in 1355 on his father's orders. When Pedro became king, legend says he had her body exhumed, crowned and seated on a throne, and made the court kiss her hand. Their tombs in Alcobaça monastery face each other, so that on the Day of Judgment, when they rise, the first thing each sees will be the other.",

  "italians:hunt": "Lorenzo de' Medici, ruler of Florence, wrote a poem around 1470, 'L'uccellagione di starne', about a morning of partridge hawking in the Tuscan hills with his friends. It is full of names of falcons and dogs, quarrels over who flew best, a lost hawk, and a hungry return to dinner. Hunting for the Florentine elite was friendship, poetry and the joy of the countryside together.",
  "italians:battle": "On 29 May 1176 the cities of the Lombard League faced Emperor Frederick Barbarossa at Legnano. The citizen infantry gathered around the Carroccio, a great ox-cart bearing the city's banner and an altar, and swore to defend it to death. They held, the Milanese cavalry returned, and the emperor was unhorsed and briefly thought dead. The free communes of Italy had won their liberty.",
  "italians:gather": "In 1338 Ambrogio Lorenzetti painted the Effects of Good Government on the walls of Siena's Palazzo Pubblico. In the countryside outside the city gate, peasants reap wheat, thresh it, drive mules loaded with sacks to town, and hunt with hawks, while a winged figure of Security watches over all. The council of the Nine wanted citizens to remember that peace brings the harvest. Ten years later the plague came.",
  "italians:ritual": "When a pope dies, the cardinals are locked in conclave to choose a successor, praying to the Holy Spirit. Outside, crowds in Saint Peter's Square watch a little chimney on the Sistine Chapel. Black smoke means no decision; white smoke and the bells mean a new pope. People wait, many in prayer, not knowing who will lead the Church into the next years.",
  "italians:rain": "Imagined: November in the Po valley, and the fog is so thick in Mantua that the boatmen will not take their barges out onto the lakes. Merchants waiting to send cheese and rice downriver to Venice sit in the taverns and complain. The innkeeper says the nebbia will lift when it wants to. On the third morning the sun burns through, and the river is suddenly crowded with sails.",
  "italians:flood": "On 4 November 1966 the Arno burst its banks in Florence. Water and mud up to six metres deep filled the streets around Santa Croce, damaging Cimabue's crucifix and millions of books. Young volunteers from Italy and around the world came to dig out the libraries and archives by hand; Florentines called them the angeli del fango, the mud angels.",
  "italians:wind": "On 25 November 1343 Petrarch was in Naples when a great storm struck the coast. He wrote to a friend that he saw the sea rise over the harbour, ships wrecked against each other, and the young queen walking barefoot to church with her ladies to pray. He vowed he would never again travel by sea if he could help it. His letter is one of the earliest eyewitness storm descriptions in Europe.",
  "italians:fire": "On 7 February 1497, carnival day in Florence, the followers of the Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola built a huge pyramid in the Piazza della Signoria and burned 'vanities': mirrors, cosmetics, playing cards, lutes, books and paintings. Children had collected them door to door. Many Florentines felt pure and saved; others were furious. Fifteen months later Savonarola himself was burned in the same square.",
  "italians:thunder": "On the night of 5 April 1492 lightning struck the lantern of the dome of Florence cathedral, and marble blocks fell toward the Medici palace. Lorenzo de' Medici, gravely ill at Careggi, asked which side had fallen, and on hearing the answer said he would die. He died three days later. Florentines took the lightning as an omen of the troubles soon to follow.",
  "italians:birth": "On 15 April 1452 Antonio da Vinci, a notary, wrote in his notebook that his grandson had been born that night at the third hour, and listed the godparents at the baptism. The child was illegitimate, son of the notary Ser Piero and a young woman named Caterina, and could not follow his father's profession. His name was Leonardo. No one in Vinci imagined what he would become.",
  "italians:death": "Raphael died in Rome on Good Friday, 6 April 1520, his thirty-seventh birthday. His unfinished painting of the Transfiguration was placed at the head of his bier, and Vasari wrote that all who saw it there wept. He was buried in the Pantheon, as he had asked. Pope Leo X wept too. Rome mourned him as if an angel had departed.",

  "sicilians:hunt": "Emperor Frederick II, king of Sicily, wrote the treatise 'De arte venandi cum avibus', The Art of Hunting with Birds, in the 1240s. He studied the falcons himself, testing ideas he had read in Aristotle, and records that he had the eyes of vultures sealed to see whether they find food by sight or smell. To him hunting was a science, and he loved his birds more than his courtiers did.",
  "sicilians:battle": "At Vespers on Easter Monday, 30 March 1282, outside the church of Santo Spirito in Palermo, a French soldier insulted a Sicilian woman, and her husband killed him. The crowd rose against the French rulers of Charles of Anjou. In the following weeks the French were killed throughout Sicily. Legend says anyone who could not pronounce 'ciciri', chickpeas, in the Sicilian way was taken for French.",
  "sicilians:gather": "Each spring at the tonnara of Favignana, the fishermen spread a maze of nets to trap the bluefin tuna migrating past. When enough fish were in the final chamber, the rais, the chief, gave the order, and the men chanted the cialoma as they hauled up the net and gaffed the great fish in the mattanza. It was hard, bloody, ancient work, and the sea's harvest for the whole island.",
  "sicilians:ritual": "In 1624, during a plague in Palermo, a woman dreamed that Saint Rosalia showed her where her bones lay in a cave on Monte Pellegrino. The bones were found and carried through the city in procession, and the plague ended. Every July since, Palermo holds the Festino of Santa Rosalia, pulling her huge chariot through the streets, asking her to protect the city in the year to come.",
  "sicilians:rain": "Sicilians tell that in the Middle Ages a terrible drought struck the island, and the people prayed to Saint Joseph for rain, promising to honour him if it came. The rain fell, and in thanks they set tables of food for the poor. Every 19 March since, families build the Tavola di San Giuseppe, an altar of breads, fava beans and sweets, and feed whoever comes to the door.",
  "sicilians:flood": "At dawn on 28 December 1908 a great earthquake struck the Strait of Messina, and minutes later a tsunami up to twelve metres high swept over the waterfronts of Messina and Reggio. Perhaps eighty thousand people died. Sailors of the Russian navy, anchored nearby, were among the first to dig out survivors. Messina was rebuilt in low houses, and has never forgotten.",
  "sicilians:wind": "Imagined: on the island of Lipari, a fisherman's wife watches the sea go white under a mistral. Her husband's boat is out. The women gather at the church of the Annunziata and pray to Saint Bartholomew, the island's patron. Toward evening a lamp shows on the sea, then a hull. She runs down to the harbour, scolding him all the way, laughing and crying at once.",
  "sicilians:fire": "In March 1669 Etna erupted, and a river of lava flowed toward Catania. Diego Pappalardo led fifty men in wet cowhides to break through the hardened crust and divert the flow. It turned toward the town of Paternò, whose armed men drove them off. The lava reached the city walls of Catania and poured into the sea. Sicilians kept praying to Saint Agatha, whose veil was carried against the fire.",
  "sicilians:thunder": "On 11 January 1693 a great earthquake struck the Val di Noto in southeast Sicily, with a roar that survivors compared to thunder from under the earth. Some sixty thousand died, and Catania, Noto, Ragusa and Modica were ruined. The Sicilians rebuilt them in a new, joyful baroque style, golden stone and curving balconies, as if to answer death with beauty.",
  "sicilians:birth": "Constance of Hauteville, heir of the Norman kings of Sicily, became pregnant at forty after nine childless years of marriage to Emperor Henry VI. Fearing doubt, she gave birth on 26 December 1194 in a tent in the public square of Jesi, with townswomen as witnesses. The child, Frederick, grew up an orphan in Palermo and became the emperor called the wonder of the world.",
  "sicilians:death": "Beneath the Capuchin convent of Palermo lie the catacombs, where from 1599 friars, then nobles and merchants, were dried and preserved, dressed in their best clothes, and placed in long rows along the walls. Families visited them, prayed, and dressed them again. The last was Rosalia Lombardo, a little girl of two who died in 1920 and still seems to be sleeping.",

  "maltese:hunt": "In 1530 Emperor Charles V granted Malta to the Knights of St John, for a yearly tribute of a single falcon, to be sent to the viceroy of Sicily on All Souls' Day. Each year a trained bird left Malta, the only rent the Knights paid for their island. Falcons, quail and turtle doves passing over in spring and autumn had always been hunted by the Maltese.",
  "maltese:battle": "From May to September 1565 the Ottoman army besieged Malta. Fort St Elmo held for a month and fell on 23 June; its defenders were killed to the last man. But Birgu and Senglea, defended by the Knights and the Maltese under Grand Master Jean de Valette, never fell. On 8 September the Turks withdrew. The Maltese still celebrate it as the Day of Victory, Il-Vittorja.",
  "maltese:gather": "Along the coast of Gozo at Xwejni, salt pans cut into the limestone have been worked for centuries. In the hot summer months families flood them with seawater and let the sun dry it; then they sweep the white salt into heaps and carry it in sacks. It is quiet, back-breaking work done the same way by grandparents and grandchildren, under the same sun.",
  "maltese:ritual": "In 1883 Karmni Grima, a woman of Għarb in Gozo, heard a woman's voice calling her as she passed the little chapel of Ta' Pinu. She went in to pray, and the voice asked her to say three Hail Marys. Later her own mother was healed after her prayers. Pilgrims have since come to Ta' Pinu to ask the Virgin about their future and leave thanks for answered prayers.",
  "maltese:rain": "Imagined: in a dry spring in the 1700s, the farmers of the Maltese countryside look at their cisterns, half empty, and at the stony terraces. They walk in procession to the parish church with the statue of the patron saint, praying for rain. For days the sky is blue and cloudless. Then a grey sky comes up from the southwest, and the first heavy drops dry on the warm stones.",
  "maltese:flood": "Imagined: an autumn cloudburst over Malta sends torrents down the narrow valleys, the widien, that cut through the island. In Birkirkara and Qormi, streets become rivers, and the water carries off carts and doors to the harbour. Neighbours pull each other up onto the flat roofs and wait. When the water passes, they sweep out the mud and say it rains in Malta only to make up for the summer.",
  "maltese:wind": "According to the Book of Acts, around AD 60 a ship carrying Saint Paul as a prisoner to Rome was caught for fourteen days by a violent northeast wind and wrecked on Malta. 'The barbarous people showed us no little kindness,' Luke writes, 'for they kindled a fire because of the rain and the cold.' Maltese people honour Paul's shipwreck on 10 February, as the arrival of their faith.",
  "maltese:fire": "In spring 1942 Malta was the most bombed place on earth. On 7 April German bombs destroyed the Royal Opera House in Valletta; fires burned in the ruins of the city. People slept in rock shelters and queued for food. On 15 April King George VI awarded the whole island the George Cross for its courage. The ruins of the opera house stood for decades, a reminder.",
  "maltese:thunder": "On 9 April 1942, during an air raid, a German bomb fell through the dome of the Rotunda of Mosta, where some three hundred people were gathered for evening prayers. It bounced across the floor and did not explode. Nobody was hurt. The people of Mosta called it a miracle of the Virgin, and a replica of the bomb is still kept in the church.",
  "maltese:birth": "On a Maltese child's first birthday the family holds the quċċija. The baby sits on the floor surrounded by objects: a rosary, a pen, a book, a coin, a hard-boiled egg, scissors. Whatever the child picks up first is said to show their future: a priest, a writer, a rich man. The family laughs and cheers, but everyone also wonders quietly what life this child will make.",
  "maltese:death": "Jean de Valette, Grand Master of the Knights and hero of the Great Siege, died on 21 August 1568 after a stroke while hunting. His body was carried by galley across the harbour to the new city that bore his name, Valletta, and buried in a chapel, later moved to the crypt of St John's Co-Cathedral. The Maltese and the Knights mourned him as the saviour of the island.",
});
